<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-histograms">
  <title>Histograms</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Describe the distribution of a quantitative variable using a histogram.</p></li>
      <li><p>Read counts and intervals from a histogram.</p></li>
      <li><p>Convert a count into a relative frequency and interpret it in context.</p></li>
      <li><p>Apply the endpoint convention for assigning values to bins.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain how bin width affects the appearance of a distribution.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>Our goal in data analysis is to describe patterns in data and build a
    useful summary of a group. A graph that summarizes the distribution of a
    variable lets us see two things at once: the possible values the variable
    takes, and how many individuals fall at each value or interval of
    values.</p>

    <p>A dotplot does this well for small data sets. A
    <term>histogram</term> is an alternative that works far better for large
    ones. A histogram divides the variable values into equal-sized intervals
    and shows how many individuals fall in each one.</p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-building-histogram">
    <title>From dots to bars</title>

    <p>Consider hip girth<mdash/>the measurement around the hips<mdash/>for
    <m>507</m> adults who exercise regularly. The measurements range from
    <m>79</m> cm to <m>128</m> cm.</p>

    <p>A dotplot of <m>507</m> values is readable but crowded: each individual
    gets its own dot, and stacks near the center become tall and hard to
    count. The histogram fixes this in two steps.</p>

    <p><em>Step one: choose bins.</em> Divide the range into equal-sized
    intervals called <term>bins</term>. Here we use a bin width of
    <m>5</m> cm, starting at <m>75</m>.</p>

    <p><em>Step two: count and draw.</em> Count how many individuals fall in
    each bin, then draw a bar whose height is that count.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-hip-histogram">
      <caption>Hip girth measurements for <m>507</m> adults who exercise
      regularly, with a bin width of <m>5</m> cm.</caption>
      <image width="85%" xml:id="image-hip-histogram">
        <description>A histogram of hip girth for 507 adults. The horizontal
        axis runs from 75 to 130 centimeters in bins of width 5. Bar heights
        rise from 2 at the left to a peak of 164 in the bin from 95 to 100
        centimeters, then fall off to the right, reaching 1 in the final bin.
        The distribution is roughly bell-shaped with a slightly longer tail on
        the right.</description>
        <asymptote>
unitsize(0.155cm, 0.034cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));

real
[] L = {75,80,85,90,95,100,105,110,115,120,125};
real
[] C = { 2, 20, 48,115,164, 97, 42, 15,  2,  1,  1};

for (int i = 0; i &lt; L.length; ++i) {
  if (C
[i] &gt; 0) {
    filldraw((L
[i],0)--(L
[i]+5,0)--(L
[i]+5,C
[i])--(L
[i],C
[i])--cycle,
             paleblue, black+0.4bp);
  }
}

draw((75,0)--(132,0), black+0.6bp);
for (real t = 75; t &lt;= 130; t += 5) {
  draw((t,0)--(t,-4), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", t), (t,-5), S);
}

draw((75,0)--(75,180), black+0.6bp);
for (real s = 0; s &lt;= 180; s += 20) {
  draw((75,s)--(74,s), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", s), (73.5,s), W);
}

label("Hip girth (cm)", (103.5,-16));
label(rotate(90)*"Frequency (count)", (65.5,90));
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>The height of each bar is the <term>count</term> (also called the
    <term>frequency</term>) of individuals in that bin. Reading the graph:
    <m>48</m> adults have hip measurements between <m>85</m> and
    <m>90</m> cm, and <m>97</m> adults fall between <m>100</m> and
    <m>105</m> cm.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-bar-height">
      <statement>
        <p>In <xref ref="fig-hip-histogram"/>, what does the height of a bar
        tell you?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>How many individuals have values falling inside that bin.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. The height is the count, or frequency, for that
            interval.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The value of the variable for individuals in that bin.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The variable value is read off the <em>horizontal</em> axis.
            The height counts individuals.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The width of the interval that bin covers.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Every bin here has the same width, <m>5</m> cm. The bars differ
            in height, not width.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The average hip girth of individuals in that bin.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>A histogram shows counts, not averages. Nothing in the graph
            reports a mean.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-histogram-vs-dotplot">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>A histogram is generally a better choice than a dotplot when the
        data set is large.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>True. A dotplot draws one mark per individual, so with hundreds or
        thousands of values the stacks become impossible to read. A histogram
        groups individuals into bins, so the number of bars stays manageable
        no matter how many individuals there are.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-endpoints">
    <title>The endpoint convention</title>

    <p>A question comes up immediately. If one adult measures exactly
    <m>90</m> cm, does that person belong to the bin from <m>85</m> to
    <m>90</m>, or the bin from <m>90</m> to <m>95</m>? Both bins name
    <m>90</m> as an endpoint, and counting the person twice<mdash/>or not at
    all<mdash/>would be wrong.</p>

    <p>Statisticians settle this with a convention, applied consistently to
    every bin.</p>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-endpoint-rule">
      <title>Which bin gets the endpoint?</title>
      <p>A bin <em>includes</em> its left-hand endpoint and
      <em>excludes</em> its right-hand endpoint.</p>
      <p>So the bin labeled <m>85</m> to <m>90</m> contains every individual
      with a value of <m>85</m> or more, up to but not including <m>90</m>.
      An adult measuring exactly <m>90</m> cm belongs to the
      <em>next</em> bin.</p>
      <p>Every histogram in this course follows this rule.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>The convention is arbitrary<mdash/>the opposite choice would work
    equally well<mdash/>but applying it consistently guarantees that every
    individual is counted exactly once.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-endpoint-bin">
      <statement>
        <p>An adult in this study has a hip girth of exactly
        <m>100.0</m> cm. Which bin counts this person?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>The bin from <m>100</m> to <m>105</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. A bin includes its left endpoint, so <m>100.0</m>
            belongs to the bin that <em>starts</em> at <m>100</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The bin from <m>95</m> to <m>100</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That bin excludes its right endpoint, so it stops just short of
            <m>100</m>. Review
            <xref ref="asm-endpoint-rule" text="title"/>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Both bins, since <m>100</m> is a shared boundary</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Counting one person twice would make the bar heights sum to
            more than <m>507</m>. The convention exists precisely to prevent
            this.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Neither bin<mdash/>boundary values are excluded</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Then this adult would vanish from the graph. Every individual
            lands in exactly one bin.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relative-frequency">
    <title>From counts to relative frequencies</title>

    <p>A count answers <q>how many,</q> but often the more useful question is
    <q>what fraction.</q> Knowing that <m>48</m> adults fall in a bin means
    little until you know whether the sample had <m>60</m> people or
    <m>6000</m>. Dividing the count by the total gives a
    <term>relative frequency</term>, usually reported as a percentage.</p>

    <table xml:id="table-hip-frequencies">
      <title>Frequency distribution of hip girth for <m>507</m> adults</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="medium">
        <row bottom="medium" header="yes">
          <cell>Bin (cm)</cell>
          <cell>Count</cell>
          <cell>Relative frequency</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><m>75</m> to <m>80</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>0.4\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>80</m> to <m>85</m></cell><cell><m>20</m></cell><cell><m>3.9\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>85</m> to <m>90</m></cell><cell><m>48</m></cell><cell><m>9.5\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>90</m> to <m>95</m></cell><cell><m>115</m></cell><cell><m>22.7\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>95</m> to <m>100</m></cell><cell><m>164</m></cell><cell><m>32.3\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>100</m> to <m>105</m></cell><cell><m>97</m></cell><cell><m>19.1\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>105</m> to <m>110</m></cell><cell><m>42</m></cell><cell><m>8.3\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>110</m> to <m>115</m></cell><cell><m>15</m></cell><cell><m>3.0\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>115</m> to <m>120</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>0.4\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>120</m> to <m>125</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>0.2\%</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>125</m> to <m>130</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>0.2\%</m></cell></row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><m>\text{Total}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>507</m></cell>
          <cell><m>100\%</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <example xml:id="ex-relative-frequency-one-bin">
      <title>Percentage in a single bin</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Approximately what percentage of the sample has hip measurements
        between <m>85</m> and <m>90</m> cm?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Of the <m>507</m> adults, <m>48</m> fall in this bin.</p>
        <me>\frac{48}{507} \approx 0.095 = 9.5\%</me>
        <p>About <m>9.5\%</m> of the adults in this sample have hip girths of
        at least <m>85</m> cm but less than <m>90</m> cm.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-sweatpants">
      <title>Percentage across several bins</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A pants manufacturer plans to produce three sizes of sweatpants.
        Size Large will fit hip girths of <m>100</m> cm or more. What
        percentage of the sample will wear size Large?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No single bin answers this, so add the counts for every bin at
        <m>100</m> cm and above:</p>
        <me>97 + 42 + 15 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 158</me>
        <me>\frac{158}{507} \approx 0.312 = 31.2\%</me>
        <p>About <m>31.2\%</m> of the adults in this sample will wear size
        Large sweatpants.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-percentages">
      <title>Spotlight on percentages</title>
      <p><em>Percent</em> means <q>per hundred.</q> A percentage describes a
      number as a fraction out of <m>100</m>.</p>
      <p><em>Step 1<mdash/>Identify the ratio.</em> Fill in the blanks:
      (a part) out of (the group). The part is the subset with the special
      characteristic you care about.</p>
      <p><em>Step 2<mdash/>Calculate.</em> Divide the part by the group size,
      then multiply by <m>100</m>.</p>
      <p><em>Step 3<mdash/>Interpret in context.</em> For every <m>100</m>
      individuals in the group, this many will have the characteristic. Name
      the group and name the characteristic<mdash/>a percentage with no
      context is not an answer.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>Applying that pattern to <xref ref="ex-sweatpants"/>: the ratio is
    <m>158</m> out of <m>507</m> adults; the calculation gives
    <m>31.2\%</m>; and the interpretation is <q>for every <m>100</m> adults in
    this sample, about <m>31</m> will wear size Large.</q></p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 4 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-compute-relfreq">
      <statement>
        <p>Using <xref ref="table-hip-frequencies"/>, approximately what
        percentage of the sample has a hip girth of at least <m>90</m> cm but
        less than <m>95</m> cm?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>About <m>22.7\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct: <m>115 \div 507 \approx 0.227</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>115\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p><m>115</m> is the count, not a percentage. A percentage can
            never exceed <m>100\%</m> when the part is a subset of the
            group.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>4.4\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This divides in the wrong direction
            (<m>507 \div 115 \approx 4.4</m>, which is not a percentage at
            all). Divide the part by the group.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>32.3\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is the bin from <m>95</m> to <m>100</m> cm. Check the row
            labels.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 5 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-cumulative">
      <statement>
        <p>Approximately what percentage of the sample has a hip girth of less
        than <m>95</m> cm?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>About <m>36.5\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Add the first four bins:
            <m>2 + 20 + 48 + 115 = 185</m>, and
            <m>185 \div 507 \approx 0.365</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>22.7\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is only the bin from <m>90</m> to <m>95</m>. <q>Less than
            <m>95</m></q> includes every bin below it as well.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>68.8\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is the percentage below <m>100</m> cm. You included one
            bin too many.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>About <m>63.5\%</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This is the complement of the right answer<mdash/>the
            percentage <em>at or above</em> <m>95</m> cm.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 6: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-interpret-percent">
      <statement>
        <p>You compute that <m>19.1\%</m> of this sample has a hip girth of at
        least <m>100</m> cm but less than <m>105</m> cm. Write one complete
        sentence interpreting that number for someone who has not seen the
        data. Name the group, name the characteristic, and make the sentence
        stand on its own.</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Something like: <q>About <m>19</m> out of every <m>100</m> adults
        in this sample of regular exercisers have a hip girth of at least
        <m>100</m> cm but less than <m>105</m> cm.</q></p>
        <p>A complete interpretation names the group (adults in this sample
        who exercise regularly), the characteristic (hip girth in that
        interval), and the size of the share. Writing only <q><m>19.1\%</m> of
        the data</q> restates the arithmetic without saying what it describes.
        Note also that this statement is about <em>this sample</em>, not about
        all adults everywhere.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-relative-axis">
    <title>Two axes, one picture</title>

    <p>Because every relative frequency comes from dividing by the same total,
    rescaling the vertical axis from counts to percentages does not change the
    shape of the histogram at all. Only the numbers on the axis change.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-hip-relative">
      <caption>The same data as <xref ref="fig-hip-histogram"/>, with the
      vertical axis showing relative frequency instead of count. The shape is
      identical.</caption>
      <image width="85%" xml:id="image-hip-relative">
        <description>A relative frequency histogram of hip girth for the same
        507 adults. The bars have exactly the same shape as the frequency
        histogram, but the vertical axis now runs from 0 to 35 percent instead
        of 0 to 180 individuals, with the tallest bar at about 32 percent.</description>
        <asymptote>
unitsize(0.155cm, 0.175cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));

real
[] L = {75,80,85,90,95,100,105,110,115,120,125};
real
[] P = {0.4,3.9,9.5,22.7,32.3,19.1,8.3,3.0,0.4,0.2,0.2};

for (int i = 0; i &lt; L.length; ++i) {
  filldraw((L
[i],0)--(L
[i]+5,0)--(L
[i]+5,P
[i])--(L
[i],P
[i])--cycle,
           palegreen, black+0.4bp);
}

draw((75,0)--(132,0), black+0.6bp);
for (real t = 75; t &lt;= 130; t += 5) {
  draw((t,0)--(t,-0.8), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", t), (t,-1.0), S);
}

draw((75,0)--(75,35), black+0.6bp);
for (real s = 0; s &lt;= 35; s += 5) {
  draw((75,s)--(74,s), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", s) + "\%", (73.5,s), W);
}

label("Hip girth (cm)", (103.5,-3.1));
label(rotate(90)*"Relative frequency", (64.5,17.5));
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>This matters most when comparing two groups of different sizes.
    Suppose you wanted to compare hip girth for these <m>507</m> exercisers
    against a sample of <m>80</m> non-exercisers. Counts would be
    useless<mdash/>every bar in the smaller group would be shorter simply
    because the group is smaller. Relative frequencies put both groups on the
    same scale, so differences in the bars mean differences in the
    distributions.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 7 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-why-relative">
      <statement>
        <p>Why would you use relative frequency rather than count when
        comparing the hip girth distributions of two samples of different
        sizes?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Counts are affected by sample size, so the larger group would
            have taller bars regardless of its shape.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Dividing by the group total removes the effect of
            sample size, leaving only the shape of the distribution to
            compare.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Relative frequency changes the shape of the histogram to make
            differences easier to see.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The shape does not change at all<mdash/>compare
            <xref ref="fig-hip-histogram"/> with
            <xref ref="fig-hip-relative"/>. Only the axis labels differ.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Counts cannot be computed when the sample sizes differ.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Counts are always computable. The problem is that they are not
            comparable across groups of different sizes.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Relative frequency is more precise than a count.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>A relative frequency is derived from the count, so it carries
            no extra precision<mdash/>rounding usually loses a little.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  
  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-when-histogram">
    <title>When is a histogram the right graph?</title>

    <p>A histogram describes the distribution of a
    <term>quantitative</term> variable<mdash/>one whose values are numbers
    that can be ordered and measured. Hip girth, exam score, household income,
    and reaction time all qualify.</p>

    <p>A <term>categorical</term> variable, whose values are labels rather
    than measurements, calls for a bar graph instead. Eye color, academic
    major, and brand of phone have no natural numeric ordering, so there is
    nothing to divide into bins.</p>

    <p>The visual difference reflects this. Histogram bars touch, because the
    bins are adjacent intervals on a continuous number line. Bar graph bars
    are drawn with gaps, because the categories are separate and their order
    on the page carries no meaning.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 9 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-variable-type">
      <statement>
        <p>A researcher records the academic major of each student in a study.
        Which graph should she use to display this variable?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>A bar graph, because major is a categorical variable.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Majors are labels, not measurements, so there are no
            intervals to bin.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>A histogram, because she can count students in each major.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Being countable is not the same as being quantitative. A
            histogram needs values that can be divided into numeric
            intervals, and <q>biology</q> cannot be placed on a number
            line.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>A histogram, after assigning each major a number.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Assigning arbitrary numbers to labels does not make the
            variable quantitative. The bins and the shape would be artifacts
            of whatever numbering was chosen.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Either one, since both display counts.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Both display counts, but a histogram also claims its horizontal
            axis is a meaningful numeric scale. For majors, that claim would
            be false.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 10: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-describe-distribution">
      <statement>
        <p>Look at <xref ref="fig-hip-histogram"/> once more. In two or three
        sentences, describe what it tells you about hip girth among these
        <m>507</m> adults. Where are most of the measurements concentrated,
        and is the picture symmetric or lopsided?</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>A good description might read: <q>Hip measurements for these
        <m>507</m> adults run from about <m>79</m> to <m>128</m> cm, with most
        values concentrated between <m>90</m> and <m>105</m> cm. The tallest
        bar covers <m>95</m> to <m>100</m> cm. The distribution is roughly
        bell-shaped but not quite symmetric: it has a longer tail stretching
        to the right, with a handful of adults measuring well above
        <m>115</m> cm and no matching group at the low end.</q></p>
        <p>The three things worth naming in any description of a distribution
        are where the values fall overall, where they cluster, and whether the
        two sides balance.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <conclusion>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>A histogram displays the distribution of a quantitative variable by
    dividing its values into equal-sized bins and drawing a bar whose height
    is the count, or frequency, of individuals in each bin. Bins include their
    left endpoint and exclude their right endpoint, so every individual is
    counted exactly once.</p>
    <p>Dividing a count by the group total gives a relative frequency, usually
    written as a percentage. Relative frequencies allow comparison between
    groups of different sizes, and rescaling the vertical axis to show them
    does not change the shape of the graph.</p>
    <p>Bin width is a choice made by whoever draws the graph. Wider bins
    smooth the distribution and hide detail; narrower bins reveal detail but
    can make random variation look meaningful.</p>
    <p>Adapted from <pubtitle>Concepts in Statistics</pubtitle>
    (SUNY / Lumen Learning), CC BY 4.0. Hip girth measurements from the body
    dimensions data of Heinz, Peterson, Johnson, and Kerk (2003).</p>
  </conclusion>
</section>